# Changes that scripts/mutate can make without altering behaviour.
# One per line: file, function, from becomes to, two spaces, and why it
# alters nothing. Recorded by function rather than by line, because a line
# number moves whenever anything above it in the file changes.
# A line here that the tests now notice is reported as out of date, so this
# file cannot quietly excuse a change made later in the same place.

internal/policy/policy.go EvaluateAt > becomes >=  picking the last of several equal ranks gives the same level as picking the first
internal/security/security.go LooksBinary > becomes >=  the length cap is applied either way at exactly the cap
internal/security/security.go ScanReader > becomes >=  truncating a line at exactly the cap gives the same line
internal/security/security.go ScanReader < becomes <=  the bound is on a submatch index that is always inside the match, so the two agree for every detector
internal/security/security.go Redact > becomes >=  same bound, same reason: len(sub) is always one more than the value group for the only detector that has one
internal/security/security.go Redact < becomes <=  the value is always found inside its own match, so the index is never negative and never zero for any detector whose value group does not start the pattern
internal/tune/tune.go highest > becomes >=  the largest value in a list is the same whether ties replace the running answer or not
internal/tune/tune.go lowest < becomes <=  the smallest value in a list is the same whether ties replace the running answer or not
internal/tune/tune.go lessThreshold > becomes >=  it sits under a guard that the two counts differ, so the two operators cannot disagree there
internal/config/config.go Hash < becomes <=  a duplicate step name is refused by Validate, so the sort never sees two equal names and the tie break is never reached
internal/state/state.go Update < becomes <=  clamping a count at zero leaves zero either way
internal/state/state.go Update > becomes >=  a peak equal to the current value is the same peak whether it is reassigned or not
internal/state/state.go RecordGate > becomes >=  trimming a list that is already exactly the limit takes the whole list, so the two agree at the boundary
internal/state/state.go safeID > becomes >=  cutting a name that is already exactly the bound leaves the same name
internal/state/state.go Lock < becomes <=  the backoff cap changes how long a retry sleeps and nothing else; both stop doubling in the same low milliseconds
internal/state/state.go Lock > becomes >=  it gives up after one fewer consecutive missing reading; both bound the loop and neither spins, so the difference is one retry
internal/state/state.go Lock > becomes >=  it differs only for a lock whose age is exactly the stale limit to the nanosecond, which no run can arrange
internal/state/state.go Lock == becomes !=  the continue after a successful clear skips one sleep; without it the next pass creates the lock anyway, one backoff later
internal/verify/verify.go Write > becomes >=  the tail buffer trims at four times the kept line count, and String keeps the last twenty non blank lines out of whatever remains, so trimming one line earlier cannot change what it returns
internal/verify/verify.go String > becomes >=  taking the last twenty of a list that is exactly twenty long is the whole list
internal/settings/settings.go sortStrings < becomes <=  it decides whether two equal strings change places, and two equal strings are the same string, so the sorted list is identical
internal/git/git.go Open || becomes &&  git cannot print a top level path and fail, and it cannot succeed printing nothing, so the two conditions are answered by the same call
internal/git/git.go RemoteDisplay || becomes &&  same shape: git remote get-url either prints a URL and succeeds or prints nothing and fails
internal/git/git.go IndexEntries < becomes <=  the tab it looks for cannot be the first byte of a record, because the mode comes before it
internal/git/git.go ShortSHA > becomes >=  a revision of exactly the fallback length is returned whole either way
internal/git/git.go StripCredentials < becomes <=  one byte is either a slash or an at sign, so the two indexes are never equal
internal/git/git.go Open == becomes !=  git resolves the working directory before it prints the top level, so the path is already resolved and resolving it again returns the same path
